[C10] FALSIFIED
  claim:    silence_signal measures whether a phrase appears near a definition.
            'This analysis is noisy.' -> scored as DEFINED via ['is']
            'The noise was measured.' -> scored as DEFINED via ['is']
            'Anisotropy appeared in the sample.' -> scored as DEFINED via ['is']
            'A misread signal.' -> scored as DEFINED via ['is']
            the test is a substring match, so 'is' fires inside analysis, noise,
            Anisotropy and misread. Any corpus in this domain is scored as
            fully defined, driving silence_signal toward 0 and suppressing every candidate.

[C11] FALSIFIED
  claim:    jargon_mismatch scores how a given phrase diverges across domains.
            the `phrase` parameter is never read in the function body: True
            it embeds each domain's whole concatenated text, so every phrase sharing
            a domain set receives an identical score
            sim.mean() spans the full matrix including the self-similarity diagonal:
              2 domains, embeddings maximally unrelated (off-diagonal 0.0): reported mismatch 0.500, ceiling 0.500
              3 domains, embeddings maximally unrelated (off-diagonal 0.0): reported mismatch 0.667, ceiling 0.667
              4 domains, embeddings maximally unrelated (off-diagonal 0.0): reported mismatch 0.750, ceiling 0.750
            for realistic non-negative sentence similarities the score is capped at
            1 - 1/n, so with the documented 2-domain case it cannot exceed 0.500 and
            contributes at most 0.200 toward the 0.4 flagging threshold.

