[NC_002] HOLDS
  claim:    With the counts unread, the CHANNEL_LOSS verdict is set by the assumed denominator.
            the same two published percentages (>= 90% direct, as low as 32%
            mediated), read at five assumed denominators:
              n=  10  realized 0.90/0.30  point loss 0.6000  -> VALUE
              n=  25  realized 0.92/0.32  point loss 0.6000  -> CHANNEL_LOSS
              n=  50  realized 0.90/0.32  point loss 0.5800  -> CHANNEL_LOSS
              n= 100  realized 0.90/0.32  point loss 0.5800  -> CHANNEL_LOSS
              n= 250  realized 0.90/0.32  point loss 0.5800  -> CHANNEL_LOSS
            verdict at n=10:  VALUE (intervals overlap: True)
            verdict at n=250: CHANNEL_LOSS (intervals overlap: False)
            nothing about the report changed across those rows. The real counts
            are UNREAD -- the publisher hosts are unreachable from this
            environment -- so the denominator is assumed, and the verdict is a
            property of that assumption.
            at the small end the POINT moves too: reconstruction error 0.0200 at n=10 against 0.0000 at n=250, because an integer
            count over a small denominator cannot represent an arbitrary
            percentage.

[NC_006] HOLDS
  claim:    The assigned-path bound is tighter than the headline bound on the same eleven runs.
            reported campaign: 11 runs, 1 find, 10 misses
            headline (any path):   0.0909 [0.0023, 0.4128]  (1/11)
            assigned path only:    0.0000 [0.0000, 0.2849]  (0/11)
            verdict kind: SINGLE_FIND
            side-path share of finds: 1.0
            the one find came off a SIDE path, so the route the campaign was
            designed around found nothing in eleven runs. Its upper bound is
            0.2849 against the headline's 0.4128.
            this bounds the PROCESS, not the result: a rarely-reached real
            result and a once-reached artifact produce the same 1/11.

